WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.700
One kind of cell, the hair cell, tells you that a violin is playing an A. A hair

2
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:14.260
cell turns a tiny movement into a graded voltage.

3
00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:20.000
But how does a coiled tube turn pitch into something a cell can read?

4
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.720
Uncoil the cochlea, and the answer runs along its length.

5
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.680
Frequency is turned into position.

6
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:34.073
Cochlear hair cells sit on the basilar membrane, which runs the length of the coil.

7
00:00:35.053 --> 00:00:39.113
Near the base, where sound enters, the membrane is narrow and stiff.

8
00:00:39.973 --> 00:00:42.813
Toward the apex it is wider and more compliant.

9
00:00:43.573 --> 00:00:47.253
So the membrane has a stiffness gradient from base to apex.

10
00:00:48.183 --> 00:00:52.683
Sound entering at the base sets up a travelling wave.

11
00:00:53.443 --> 00:00:59.483
It moves from base to apex, and grows until it reaches the place whose stiffness

12
00:00:59.483 --> 00:01:01.043
suits its frequency.

13
00:01:01.943 --> 00:01:04.903
There it peaks, and then it dies away.

14
00:01:06.163 --> 00:01:08.303
High frequencies peak near the base.

15
00:01:09.243 --> 00:01:11.423
Low frequencies peak near the apex.

16
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:17.867
Greenwood's function gives the place of each frequency in the human cochlea.

17
00:01:18.587 --> 00:01:21.187
Assume a cochlea 35 millimeters long.

18
00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:28.927
A tone of 1,000 Hz peaks 14 millimeters from the apex, which is 21 millimeters from

19
00:01:28.927 --> 00:01:35.927
the base. A tone of 4,000 Hz peaks 11.7 millimeters from the base, nearer than the

20
00:01:35.927 --> 00:01:42.547
lower tone. These two tones, two octaves apart, sit 9.3 millimeters apart.

21
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:49.940
Von Békésy measured these waves, many of them in human cadaver cochleas. His peaks

22
00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.820
were far broader than the tuning of a living ear.

23
00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:55.360
The difference is an amplifier.

24
00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:01.160
Outer hair cells change length when their voltage changes, and on each cycle of a

25
00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:03.780
sound they push on the membrane in step.

26
00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:09.000
This adds energy at the peak, which sharpens tuning and raises sensitivity.

27
00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:14.490
This map of frequency onto position is called tonotopy.

28
00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:21.150
The auditory nerve ends in the cochlear nucleus, then come the superior olivary

29
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.710
complex and the inferior colliculus.

30
00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:28.910
The medial geniculate nucleus then relays to auditory cortex.

31
00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:37.190
Keep this rule: frequency becomes position, and the map is kept at every relay up to cortex.
